INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '2664ccbc-64f3-5f01-aacb-a3fe6d55ef0c','Dubbing adapter','In dubbing, the professional — also called the dialogue writer or adaptor — who reworks the raw translation of a script into speakable target-language dialogue that fits the synchronization constraints of dubbing (isochrony, lip and phonetic synchrony, and kinesic synchrony) while preserving meaning, register, and characterization.','A dubbing adapter rewrites the translated lines so they fit the actors'' mouths and timing. They keep the meaning but change the words to match the lip movements. Think of it as reshaping a sentence to fit a set space.','The role exists because a literal translation rarely matches the length, stress, and lip movements on screen; the adapter makes the translated dialogue both accurate and performable in sync.','Working from a translation and the picture, the adapter rewrites each line to match the on-screen mouth movements, pauses, and gestures, marking sync symbols for the dubbing actors and director.','Dubbing Roles',ARRAY['Dubbing script adaptation','Isochrony','Lip sync','Dubbing director','Kinesic synchrony']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing the adapter with the translator; adaptation is a distinct step after the raw translation.','Prioritizing literal accuracy over lip sync, producing lines that cannot be spoken in time.','Ignoring register or characterization while chasing sync, so the dialogue sounds wrong for the character.']::text[],ARRAY['Reworking a translated film script so lines fit close-up lip movements.','Adjusting line length so the dubbed dialogue matches the original''s timing.','Marking a script with sync cues for the dubbing session.']::text[],'a1c98209-bbc4-4049-baa6-a2a7c9ee0c66'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='2664ccbc-64f3-5f01-aacb-a3fe6d55ef0c' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Dubbing adapter'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
